[
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-01",
    "question": "<p>(a) Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span>,</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r(r+3)=\\frac{1}{3} n(n+1)(n+5)\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p><p>(b) A sequence of positive integers is defined by</p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{1}=1,\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}=u_{n}+n(3n+1),\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span></p><p>Prove by induction that</p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n}=n^{2}(n-1)+1,\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>In the summation proof the next term is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+1)(k+4)\"></span>; extracting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> leaves a quadratic that factors as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+2)(k+6)\"></span>. In the recurrence proof the supplied increment joins two successive closed forms, so expand the difference and compare with that increment.</p>",
      "<p>(a) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> both sides are 4. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_k=k(k+1)(k+5)/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Add <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+1)(k+4)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The result is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+1)[k(k+5)+3(k+4)]/3=(k+1)(k+2)(k+6)/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>This is the formula for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>, so induction proves it for all positive integers. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the expression equals 1. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=k^2(k-1)+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substituting into the recurrence gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=k^2(k-1)+1+k(3k+1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Collecting gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k^3+2k^2+k+1=k(k+1)^2+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This proves the induction step and therefore the claim. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 11 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-01-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2013 · Q8, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-01-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2013 · Q8, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-01-ms-03.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2013 · Q8, extract 3\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-02",
    "question": "<p>Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span> f(n) = <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^{2n-1}+3^{2n-1}\"></span> is divisible by 5. <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The powers acquire multipliers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"9\"></span> as the index increases. Subtracting four copies of the old expression cancels one power and leaves a coefficient <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"9-4=5\"></span> on the other. The hypothesis then supplies the second multiple of five.</p>",
      "<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(1)=2+3=5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k)=5j\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"j\"></span> is an integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> the two summands acquire factors 4 and 9. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Thus <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=4f(k)+5\\cdot3^{2k-1}=5(4j+3^{2k-1})\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>This is divisible by 5, completing the induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 6 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-02-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2012 · Q10, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-04",
    "question": "<p>A sequence can be described by the recurrence formula <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}=2 u_{n}+1,\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\geqslant1, \\, u_{1}=1\"></span></p><p>(a) Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{3}\"></span>.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Prove by induction that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n}=2^{n}-1\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The first two new terms suggest a simpler sequence if you add one: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}+1=2(u_n+1)\"></span>. Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_1+1=2\"></span>, these shifted values double at each step. This explains the proposed formula before the induction establishes it.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_2=2(1)+1=3\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_3=2(3)+1=7\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) For <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^1-1=1=u_1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=2^k-1\"></span> for a positive integer <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitution into the given recurrence gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=2(2^k-1)+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This equals <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^{k+1}-1\"></span>, the required expression at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>By induction, the result holds for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The two generated terms also check the indexing of the formula: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^2-1=3\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2^3-1=7\"></span>. The induction step uses the recurrence itself, which is what takes this numerical pattern beyond the first few examples.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-04-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2012 · Q7, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-05",
    "question": "<p>Prove by induction, that for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span>,</p><p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left( \\begin{array}{cc} 3 &amp; 0 \\\\ 6 &amp; 1 \\end{array} \\right)^{n}=\\left( \\begin{array}{cc} 3^{n} &amp; 0 \\\\ 3(3^{n}-1) &amp; 1 \\end{array} \\right),\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p><p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(n)=7^{2n-1}+5\"></span> is divisible by 12.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Postmultiplication by the given matrix leaves the upper-right entry zero, so the lower-left dot product is the main algebraic check. In the divisibility proof increasing the exponent by two multiplies the power by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"49\"></span>. Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"49-1=48\"></span> is divisible by 12, the property propagates.</p>",
      "<p>(a) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the proposed power equals the original matrix. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume the expression at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=k\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Postmultiply by the original matrix. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The top row becomes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3^{k+1},0)\"></span>, and the bottom row is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(9(3^k-1)+6,1)=(3(3^{k+1}-1),1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>This proves the result at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> and hence for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(1)=12\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k)=12j\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>, the power of 7 is multiplied by 49. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=49f(k)-240=12(49j-20)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The base case and implication establish the divisibility. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 12 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-05-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2011 · Q9, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-05-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2011 · Q9, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-06",
    "question": "<p>A sequence of numbers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}, u_{4}, \\dots\"></span> is defined by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}=4 u_{n}+2, \\, u_{1}=2\"></span> Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n \\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n}=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(4^{n}-1\\right)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The denominator three can be kept throughout the induction step. Convert the added <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6/3\"></span>, then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8(4^k-1)+6=8\\cdot4^k-2\"></span>. Extracting two reveals the proposed expression with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> in its exponent.</p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the expression gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(4-1)/3=2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=2(4^k-1)/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitute: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=8(4^k-1)/3+2=(8\\cdot4^k-2)/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(4^{k+1}-1)/3\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Induction proves the claim. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 5 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-06-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2011 · Q9, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-07",
    "question": "<div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(n)=2^{n}+6^{n}\"></div><p>(a) Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(k+1)=6\\mathrm{f}(k)-4(2^{k}).\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p><p>(b) Hence, or otherwise, prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{f}(n)\"></span> is divisible by 8.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The term <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\\cdot2^k\"></span> is a multiple of eight because <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\\ge1\"></span>. Together with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6f(k)\"></span>, which is already a multiple by the hypothesis, this proves divisibility at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>. The lower bound on <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span> is used at exactly this point.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6f(k)=6\\cdot2^k+6^{k+1}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Subtract <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\\cdot2^k\"></span> to get <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\cdot2^k+6^{k+1}=f(k+1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(1)=8\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k)=8j\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=48j-8\\cdot2^{k-1}\"></span> is divisible by 8 for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\\ge1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Induction proves the result. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-07-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 · Q7, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-09",
    "question": "<p>A sequence of numbers is defined by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{1}=2,\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}=5u_{n}-4,\"></span> n <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\geqslant1.\"></span></p><p>Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+},\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n}=5^{n-1}+1.\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Subtract one from the recurrence to see that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}-1=5(u_k-1)\"></span>. The shifted first term equals one, so the exponent counts the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k-1\"></span> steps from the initial term. This explains the index appearing in the induction hypothesis.</p>",
      "<p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5^0+1=2=u_1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=5^{k-1}+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The recurrence gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=5(5^{k-1}+1)-4=5^k+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The formula now follows for every positive integer by induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 4 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-09-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2010 · Q3, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-11",
    "question": "<p>Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span>,</p><p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(n)=5^{n}+8n+3\"></span> is divisible by 4,</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(7)</span></p><p>(b) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left( \\begin{array}{cc} 3&amp;-2\\\\ 2&amp;-1 \\end{array} \\right)^{n}=\\left( \\begin{array}{cc} 2n+1&amp;-2n\\\\ 2n&amp;1-2n \\end{array} \\right)\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(7)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Choose the multiplier five in the first proof to eliminate <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5^{k+1}\"></span> on subtraction. The remaining linear expression is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-4(8k+1)\"></span>, whose divisibility is immediate. The matrix proof uses ordinary row-by-column multiplication, with each resulting entry compared against the proposed value at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(1)=5+8+3=16\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k)=4j\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=5^{k+1}+8k+11\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Subtract <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5f(k)=5^{k+1}+40k+15\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The difference is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-32k-4=-4(8k+1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>So <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"f(k+1)=4(5j-8k-1)\"></span> is divisible by 4. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This establishes the result by induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Substituting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> gives the original matrix. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume the proposed power for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=k\"></span> and multiply it by the original matrix. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The top row becomes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3(2k+1)-4k,-2(2k+1)+2k)=(2k+3,-2k-2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The bottom row is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(6k+2(1-2k),-4k-(1-2k))=(2k+2,-2k-1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>These are precisely the entries at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The result follows by induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 14 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-11-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2009 · Q8, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-12",
    "question": "<p>Prove by induction that, for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{+}\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{r(r+1)}=\\frac{n}{n+1}\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The new denominator is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+1)(k+2)\"></span>. Expressing the hypothesised sum and the extra term over this denominator produces <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k(k+2)+1=(k+1)^2\"></span>. Cancelling one <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> recovers the formula at the next index.</p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> both sides equal <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_k=k/(k+1)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Adding the next term gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k/(k+1)+1/[(k+1)(k+2)]\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The numerator is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k(k+2)+1=(k+1)^2\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=(k+1)/(k+2)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The induction establishes the formula. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 5 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev21-12-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2009 · Q4, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev21-13",
    "question": "<p>A series of positive integers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}, \\dots\"></span> is defined by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{1}=6\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n+1}=6 u_{n}-5\"></span> , for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\geqslant1.\"></span></p><p>Prove by induction that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{n}=5\\times 6^{n-1}+1\"></span> , for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\\geqslant1.\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The recurrence multiplies by six and then subtracts five, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}-1=6(u_k-1)\"></span>. The initial difference <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_1-1=5\"></span> supplies the coefficient in the formula. This gives a way to predict the closed form whose induction step follows.</p>",
      "<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the expression gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5\\cdot1+1=6\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_k=5\\cdot6^{k-1}+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u_{k+1}=6(5\\cdot6^{k-1}+1)-5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This equals <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"5\\cdot6^k+1\"></span>, the proposed next term. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The claim follows by induction. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 5 marks.</p>",
